  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 157 PM CDT Mon Sep 21 2026\n\nZonal upper flow with weak surface high pressure to the north \nkeeping conditions rather benign and much more fall like than \nrecent weeks. Low stratus was slowly diminishing with a moderate \ninversion over the boundary layer on the 12Z sounding. Conditions \nonly slowly change into early Wednesday so stratus returning and \nbuilding down again tonight is anticipated as per most hi-res \nmodels. Some patchy drizzle could return late tonight into Tuesday \nmorning with slow clearing again for highs in the upper 60s to mid \n70s. Modest southerly flow off the surface returns Wednesday for \nsomewhat better mixing and less stratus concern as upper heights \nslowly rise under a building south-central CONUS ridge. \n\nMid and upper levels moisten considerably late Wednesday into Friday \nas the upper ridge nudges east. Precipitable water values reach \naround 1.7 inches though forcing for ascent is not strong. Moderate \nNBM precipitation chances seem reasonable in this pattern with \nlittle concern for any heavy amounts or shear/instability to support \nsevere convection. A northern states wave should push the greater \nmoisture east of the area by late in the weekend. Temperature \nspecifics are hard to nail down with warmer temperatures outside of \nprecipitation but some 80s for highs seem likely for at least Sunday \nand Monday.",
